On each subinterval $[x_(j-1), x_j]$, the function $overline(f_j)$ differs from $eval(f)_[x_(j-1), x_j]$ only at the two endpoints, so by the finite-change lemma $eval(f)_[x_(j-1), x_j]$ is integrable and

$
  integral_(x_(j-1))^(x_j) f = integral_(x_(j-1))^(x_j) overline(f_j).
$

Each $overline(f_j)$ is continuous on a closed bounded interval, hence integrable. Additivity of the integral over adjacent subintervals assembles these pieces into the displayed formula.
